8 :M.053 .005(The key has to be generated, often depending on one or more security parameters \(e.g.,)J96 513 :M1.026 .103(one for the cryptographic security of a pseudorandom sequence and another for the)J96 528 :M1.666 .167(collusion size\). The standard case where the same key is used in embedding and)J96 543 :M-.142(extracting is called symmetric.)A79 564 :M.67(E.)A96 564 :M.939 .094(An entity or person that embeds and extracts is called an embeddor or an extractor,)J96 579 :M-.081(respectively.)A79 611 :Mf1_14 sf3.926 .393(Differences Between the Models)J79 634 :Mf0_12 sf.846 .085(Noticing differences between models is often equally important as noticing similarities.)J79 649 :M.769 .077(Hence I also sketch some points where the various applications differ once we consider)J79 664 :M-.099(more than the core components. These differences are not all independent.)A79 685 :M<A5>S96 685 :M-.073(Is there a given covermessage, and if yes, does it have to be used several times?)A113 700 :M.632 .063(For instance, in fingerprinting there is typically the original image or music to be)J96 715 :M-.032(sold. In contrast, in steganography, the covermessage can often be designed specifically)A96 730 :M-.098(for hiding the embedded message, or the stegomessage may be constructed immediately.)A113 745 :M.222 .022(If the same covermessage is used several times with different embedded messages,)J96 760 :M-.083(collusion attacks have to be considered. This is typical for fingerprinting.)Aendp%%Page: 3 3%%BeginPageSetupinitializepage(Birgit Pfitzmann; page: 3 of 4)setjob%%EndPageSetup-13 -12 :TgS 13 12 568 818 rC304 46 :Mf0_10 sf.417 .042<D1203320D1>J79 88 :Mf0_12 sf<A5>S96 88 :M1.341 .134(Does the covermessage play any role in extracting, too? In the second figure, two)J96 103 :M-.06(dashed arrows represent the cases where it is an input or an output, respectively.)A113 118 :M1.313 .131(For instance, in fingerprinting, the covermessage can be an input in extracting,)J96 133 :M.921 .092(because the merchant is both the embeddor and the extractor. In steganography, the)J96 148 :M.332 .033(covermessage is usually neither an input nor an output, but some situations may be so)J96 163 :M-.001(restricted \(e.g., war letters home\) that the embeddor cannot afford to lose the legitimate)A96 178 :M-.068(bandwidth offered, and thus the covermessage must be extracted.)A79 199 :M<A5>S96 199 :M-.156(What information should the stegoanalyst get legitimately?)A113 214 :M1.824 .182(For instance, in fingerprinting, the stegoanalyst is the buyer and pays for the)J96 229 :M-.018(stegomessage. Thus the stegomessage has to be quite similar to the covermessage, even)A96 244 :M.29 .029(if the stegoanalyst does not know much about the covermessage and cannot notice the)J96 259 :M-.087(difference. Treaty verification and steganography have no such goal.)A79 280 :M<A5>S96 280 :M-.13(What is the adversarial goal of the stegoanalyst:)A96 298 :M<A5>S113 298 :M-.128(to detect stegomessages;)A96 316 :M<A5>S113 316 :M-.123(to find out the embedded message;)A96 334 :M<A5>S113 334 :M-.08(to prove that a message has been embedded, or even what message, to third parties;)A96 352 :M<A5>S113 352 :M1.326 .133(to remove the embedded message from the stegomessage without changing the)J113 367 :M.942 .094(stegomessage too much, e.g., retaining the similarity to the covermessage \(as an)J113 382 :M.511 .051(illegal redistributor might\) or even keeping the active interference unnoticed \(as a)J113 397 :M.29 .029(censor might who wants to conceal his existence, or a telecommunication provider)J113 412 :M-.128(who claims to guarantee a transparent channel of a certain bandwidth\);)A96 430 :M<A5>S113 430 :M.181 .018(or to jam, i.e., to remove all possible embedded messages without much care about)J113 445 :M-.142(the covermessage?)A96 466 :M.658 .066(The first three goals will usually be achieved by passive observation, the last two by)J96 481 :M-.154(active interference.)A79 502 :M<A5>S96 502 :M.483 .048(What does the stegoanalyst know about the covermessage a priori? He might know it)J96 517 :M.286 .029(entirely, or a probability space that it is chosen from, or a family of probability spaces)J96 532 :M1.552 .155(indexed by external events \(e.g., that a letter \322it is raining\323 is less likely in good)J96 547 :M-.075(weather\), or some predicate about it. This knowledge will often be quite fuzzy.)A79 579 :Mf1_14 sf3.878 .388(More Examples)J79 602 :Mf0_12 sf.898 .09(So far, the examples were mainly for fingerprinting and steganography. Here are a few)J79 617 :M-.247(more:)A79 638 :M<A5>S96 638 :M-.06(Covert channels are similar to steganography, but the coverdata are the whole behaviour)A96 653 :M1.163 .116(of a system. This fact also gives the stegoanalyst more freedom in being active, in)J96 668 :M-.121(particular in designing the probability space of the coverdata in the first place.)A79 689 :M<A5>S96 689 :M1.574 .157(Subliminal channels in authentication protocols are interesting in cases where the)J96 704 :M-.125(message to be authenticated is completely known to the stegoanalyst and the stegoanalyst)A96 719 :M2.419 .242(may jam wrong messages. In these cases, the only available coverdata are the)J96 734 :M.365 .036(authentication data. Here the stegoanalyst has a precise criterion, valid authentication,)J96 749 :M-.124(and may change the stegodata as long as valid authentications remain valid.)Aendp%%Page: 4 4%%BeginPageSetupinitializepage(Birgit Pfitzmann; page: 4 of 4)setjob%%EndPageSetup-13 -12 :TgS 13 12 568 818 rC304 46 :Mf0_10 sf.417 .042<D1203420D1>J79 88 :Mf0_12 sf<A5>S96 88 :M.575 .058(Traitor tracing for broadcast encryption is a kind of fingerprinting of a cryptographic)J96 103 :M.538 .054(key, i.e., the coverdata are a key needed to decrypt the ciphertexts that are broadcast.)J96 118 :M-.021(The stegodata must retain this property, and so must the data the stegoanalyst produces,)A96 133 :M.089 .009(i.e., the keys for pirate decoders.)J79 154 :M<A5>S96 154 :M1.917 .192(Avoiding traffic analysis means hiding locations or addresses. This is somewhat)J96 169 :M-.014(different from the other examples in that \322location\323 is not a message. Some schemes do)A96 184 :M.209 .021(work by hiding messages in continuous dummy traffic, but even those are often multi-)J96 199 :M-.037(party protocols, i.e., many embeddors must send their \322stegomessages\323 in a coordinated)A96 214 :M-.064(way. I propose not to use the terminology in this application.)A79 235 :M1.432 .143(One simple conclusion can also be drawn: If the covermessage can be chosen by the)J79 250 :M.047 .005(embeddor, and the stegoanalyst does not know it completely and is not allowed to jam, the)J79 265 :M1.144 .114(embeddor and extractor can always defeat the stegoanalyst by encoding the embedded)J79 280 :M-.061(message within their legitimate choice among covermessages. Of course, this is not quite so)A79 295 :M-.054(clear if, in practice, none of the parties has a formal probabilistic model of the source. But it)A79 310 :M.714 .071(still shows that some steganography is possible whenever any normal communication is)J79 325 :M-.12(possible. More complicated statements are treated in the individual papers.)Aendp%%Trailerend%%EOF